course: geography
lesson: 43
title: Moving continents: from Pangaea to today
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: If you could rewind the Earth like a film, you would see the continents slide together until, about 300-200 million years ago, almost all the land was one giant supercontinent called Pangaea, ringed by one world ocean, Panthalassa.
explain: Then Pangaea tore apart, first into Laurasia in the north and Gondwana in the south, and the pieces drifted to where they are now, a few centimetres a year.
explain: Look at a world map on your bedroom wall and slide South America over to Africa: the bulge of Brazil tucks into the curve of West Africa like two jigsaw pieces.
explain: The same fossil reptiles turn up on continents now oceans apart, and Britain has coal from tropical swamps and Antarctica has fossil forests, because those lands once lay somewhere else.
explain: The film keeps playing: the Atlantic is still widening, Africa is creeping into Europe, and in a couple of hundred million years the continents will probably meet again, though scientists argue about the shape.
fact: About 300-200 million years ago almost all land was joined in one supercontinent called Pangaea.
fact: Pangaea was surrounded by one giant ocean called Panthalassa.
fact: Dinosaurs lived on Pangaea, but humans appeared only a few hundred thousand years ago, long after it split.
fact: Pangaea began to break up about 200 million years ago, first into Laurasia in the north and Gondwana in the south.
fact: The coasts of South America and Africa fit together like jigsaw pieces, and the fit is even better along the edges of the continental shelves under the sea, because today's beaches have been worn away and flooded by the sea.
fact: Fossils of the same reptiles, Mesosaurus and Lystrosaurus, are found on continents now separated by oceans that these animals could never have crossed.
fact: The Atlantic Ocean is a young ocean that started opening about 180 million years ago and is still widening.
fact: India broke away from Gondwana and raced north at up to about 15 cm a year (today it moves about 5 cm a year), hitting Asia about 50 million years ago and pushing up the Himalayas.
fact: Pangaea was not the first supercontinent: earlier ones such as Rodinia, about 1 billion years ago, came together and split too.
fact: Scientists predict the continents will join again in about 200-250 million years, though they argue about the exact shape.
fact: Africa is pushing slowly north into Europe, squeezing the Mediterranean, which may close in tens of millions of years.
fact: Moving continents changed climates: Antarctica once had forests, and coal is found under Britain, which lay near the equator about 300 million years ago.
exercise: What was Pangaea? | A supercontinent joining almost all the land | The very first ocean on Earth, before any land rose | A huge volcano in the middle of Africa | An ancient name for Europe
exercise: When was almost all land joined in Pangaea? | About 300-200 million years ago | About 3,000 years ago, in the Bronze Age | About 65 million years ago, with the dinosaurs | About 4 billion years ago, soon after Earth formed
exercise: What was the giant ocean around Pangaea called? | Panthalassa | The Pacifica Sea | The Gondwana Sea | The Atlantis Ocean
exercise: Into which two big pieces did Pangaea first split? | Laurasia in the north and Gondwana in the south | Europe in the north and Africa with India in the south | Rodinia in the north and Asia in the south | America in the west and Eurasia in the east
exercise: When did Pangaea begin to break up? | About 200 million years ago | About 2 million years ago | About 2,000 years ago, in Roman times | About 2 billion years ago
exercise: Which two coasts fit together like jigsaw pieces? | South America and Africa | Australia and the coast of Greenland | North America and eastern Asia | Europe and the coast of Antarctica
exercise: Why are Mesosaurus fossils on two continents a clue? | These reptiles could not cross an ocean, so the lands were joined | The reptiles were carried across the ocean by early sailors in their boats | They flew between the continents | The fossils were moved there by museums
exercise: How old is the Atlantic Ocean? | Young: it began opening about 180 million years ago | As old as the Earth itself, about 4.5 billion years | Only about 10,000 years old | About 1 billion years old, like Rodinia
exercise: Is the Atlantic Ocean getting wider or narrower? | Wider, a few centimetres a year | Narrower, a few centimetres a year | Neither, it never changes | Narrower, about a kilometre a year
exercise: What happened when India hit Asia about 50 million years ago? | The crust crumpled up into the Himalayas | India sank under the sea and came up again | The Atlantic Ocean opened | Pangaea was formed
exercise: Was Pangaea the first supercontinent? | No, earlier ones such as Rodinia formed and split too | Yes, the land was always together before it | Yes, there has only ever been one | No, but it was the only one with dinosaurs on it
exercise: What do scientists predict for the far future? | The continents will probably join again in about 200-250 million years | The continents will stop moving within the next hundred years or so, and stay put for ever | All land will sink under the sea when the ice melts | The Earth will split into two planets
exercise: Do scientists agree on the shape of the next supercontinent? | No, they argue about the exact shape | Yes, it will be exactly Pangaea again | Yes, it will be a perfect ring | No, because the continents have stopped moving
exercise: What is happening to the Mediterranean Sea? | Africa is squeezing it, and it may close in tens of millions of years | It is widening into a new ocean | It is filling up with river mud from the Nile and the Rhône, and will be dry land by next year | Nothing, it has always had the same shape
exercise: Why is there coal under Britain? | Britain once lay near the equator with steamy swamp forests | Coal was brought by glaciers from Norway in the last Ice Age, in huge loads | Coal forms only under cold countries | Ships dumped it there in Victorian times
exercise: Why does Antarctica have fossil forests? | It was once in a warmer place and climate | Trees still grow under the thick ice today | Explorers planted them there in the early 1900s | Birds dropped the seeds and fossils there long ago
test: Your friend says: "The continents have always been where they are now, maps never change." Put her right. | They drift a few cm a year, and once were joined in Pangaea | She's right, only the oceans move | They move, but only in big earthquakes, a whole kilometre at a time | They used to be much further apart than today
test: "if pangaea was still a thing could i walk from london to new york?" | Yes, the land was joined, though deserts and mountains would be in the way | No, London and New York were on different planets back then | No, there was no land at all back then | Yes, but only by swimming across Panthalassa
test: A cartoon shows dinosaurs watching humans build a city on Pangaea. What's wrong? | Humans appeared only a few hundred thousand years ago, long after Pangaea | Pangaea was too cold for dinosaurs | Nothing, humans and dinosaurs lived on Pangaea together for millions of years | Dinosaurs lived only after the continents split
test: A geologist finds the same 270-million-year-old reptile fossil in Brazil and South Africa. What is the best explanation? | The two lands were joined when the reptile lived | The reptile swam right across the wide Atlantic Ocean | The fossil was copied by a museum | The reptile lived everywhere on Earth at once
test: Why can't the jigsaw fit of South America and Africa be perfect along today's beaches? | Coasts have been worn and flooded; the shelves under the sea fit better | The continents shrank in the cold of the last Ice Age, so they no longer quite meet | Someone measured the maps wrongly | The pieces were never joined at all
test: What would happen to the Mediterranean if Africa kept pushing north for tens of millions of years? | It could close and new mountains could rise where it was | It would grow into a huge ocean | It would turn into a desert by next century, as the Sahara spreads north | Nothing, Africa is moving south
test: India moves about 5 cm a year now. "hang on, how did it get from near Antarctica to Asia then?" | It raced north faster, up to about 15 cm a year, for tens of millions of years | It was towed by strong sea currents in just a few years | It was blown there by a giant volcano | It sank and came back up in Asia
test: Tom says: "Coal under Britain proves Britain used to be a jungle in the same place." What's the best correction? | Britain was a swampy forest, but because it lay near the equator then | He's right, the equator itself moved north to Britain | No, coal comes from old ice, not plants | No, coal was made by volcanoes only
test: A clever puzzle: the Atlantic opens a few cm a year and is about 5,000 km wide. Roughly how long did it take to open? | About 100-200 million years | About 1,000 years, since the Vikings | About 50,000 years, give or take | About 10 billion years, longer than the Earth
test: Mia thinks Pangaea was the only supercontinent ever. What's true? | Supercontinents form and break up again and again, like Rodinia before it | Pangaea was the first and there will never be another one like it | There were never any supercontinents, it is a myth | Rodinia came after Pangaea broke up
test: "so in 250 million yrs it's all one big blob of land again, right, 100% sure?" | Probably, but scientists still argue about the exact shape | Yes, exactly like Pangaea again, and every scientist is certain of it | No, the continents will all sink | No, the continents will stop for good
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the supercontinent Pangaea? | A landmass comprising almost all continental crust, extant some 300-200 million years ago | An oceanic basin that occupied the present position of the Pacific | A landmass that formed approximately fifty million years ago in Asia | The final supercontinent, after which no other shall form
exam: Identify the evidence that is consistent with the hypothesis that the continents were formerly contiguous. | Identical fossil reptiles upon continents now separated by oceans | The present equality of the areas of all continents | The absence of any mountain ranges upon the continents | The identical climate prevailing upon every continent at the present day
exam: It is given that substantial deposits of coal are present beneath Great Britain. Identify the principal reason for their formation. | Britain formerly lay near the equator and supported swamp forests | Coal was transported thither by glaciers from Scandinavia | Coal forms exclusively beneath regions of cold climate | Coal was deposited by volcanic eruptions in historic times
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the Atlantic Ocean. | It began to open approximately 180 million years ago and continues to widen | It is coeval with the Earth and has not altered in width | It is presently contracting at a rate of several metres annually as Africa advances | It came into existence within the last ten thousand years
